  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer to "is archaeology a good career"</h2>

  <p>
    The work is real and there is a lot of it: thousands of protected sites, many digs to document, many objects to conserve. The weak point is money and paid seats. The people who can pay you are few, and most of them are public bodies.
  </p>

  <p>
    Archaeology has two filters, not one. The first is getting in: the right master's degree, an entrance exam or a notice with a small number of posts. The second is moving up or staying funded: a promotion queue that has stalled, or a project that ends in a year.
  </p>

  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Most pages on this topic list jobs and a salary range. Few show the numbers behind the jobs: how many posts, how many empty, how many people qualify, how long a project lasts. Judge archaeology by those numbers, and by whether you would still want the work in a hot, dull week of recording.
    </p>
  </div>

