  <h2 id="who-pays">Who pays, and how they find you</h2>

  <p>
    Every career has a layer of who pays and how they trust you. In archaeology the answer changes by route, and most routes are employer-only.
  </p>

  <div class="feature-grid">
    {whoPaysCards.map((item) => (
      <div class="feature-card">
        <div class="feature-card__label">{item.label}</div>
        <div class="feature-card__title">{item.title}</div>
        <p>{item.body}</p>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <p>
    <strong>The first basic skill to learn is proof.</strong> A one-page sample survey of a local site, with a clear scope and your method, is your offer in a form a client can check. Then follow up with one specific question, ask the first happy client for a referral, and have one honest price conversation in writing. The number worth watching is how many proposals or sample surveys turn into a paid pilot.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Building near a monument creates a paperwork market.</strong> Under the 1958 Act, construction is banned in the 100 metre prohibited area around a centrally protected monument, and needs permission in the 200 metre regulated area. The National Monuments Authority takes permission applications through an online portal (from 1 July 2025) and links a Heritage Impact Assessment advisory on its site. We found no data on what consultants earn from this, so treat it as a place to test one small paid pilot, not as a promise.
  </p>

  <h2 id="digital">The digital and science layer</h2>

  <p>
    Heritage documentation is moving to drones, laser scanning and GIS. A 2024 preprint described a temple gateway in Karnataka recorded with drone and ground photogrammetry and terrestrial laser scanning. A vendor case study described a pilot LiDAR scan of an 11th-century temple in Telangana. An Esri India article says drone surveys have been done in several ASI circles, but it gave no document reference, so we treat that as unverified.
  </p>

  <p>
    A second gap is science. An archaeobotanist at the Birbal Sahni Institute of Palaeosciences has argued since 2022 for a centre for archaeological sciences, with labs for archaeobotany, animal remains, residue analysis, stable isotopes and ancient DNA. He says ASI relies largely on outside institutions to check samples. He told ThePrint in August 2026 that his proposal has not been taken seriously.
  </p>

  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>
      <strong>Core skill, multiplier skill, proof.</strong> Field recording is the core. A multiplier such as GIS, 3D capture or a lab method makes you hireable outside one employer. The proof is one documented site, one model and one report that another person can check. This is the combination that makes decisions in this field pay.
    </p>
  </div>

